      "math_academy_id": "q-142946",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(-3,4),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-1,{5}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(1,{3}^{2})\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3},\\,{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(-3,4),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-1,{5}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(1,{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =-3+(-1)+1 \\\\  & =-3, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =4+{5}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\  & =4+25+9 \\\\  & =38.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} & ∼N(-3,38).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
